in of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162636/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162636"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12350"] :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{t+3}$ is $t \\in [-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3-t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\dfrac{1}{t+1}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/assets/9577ea515e58e4db49ee5a5ef9f923bc8b34e2dc232b904c956cc1b778141f14.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in [-3,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12362"] :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\ln{t}$ is $t \\in (0, \\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\dfrac{1}{t-2}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,2)\\cup(2,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{2t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076578/assets/2a4931e8dc80192219736fc4dbc586ad2405bbb628d75bd0e17fa9616567d793.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
